---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import SpecStrip from '../components/SpecStrip.astro';
import Command from '../components/Command.astro';
import RegionDiagram from '../components/RegionDiagram.astro';
import { currentVersion } from '../lib/releases';
import { BINDINGS } from '../lib/commands';

// The newest PUBLISHED STABLE entry. An unpublished release (written at release
// prep, before the tag is approved) and a beta can therefore never become the
// version the site advertises, which would otherwise point at a build
// /download does not serve.
const releases = (await getCollection('changelog')).map((e) => e.data);
const version = currentVersion(releases);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Orthant',
  description: 'Grid-based window manager for macOS.',
  applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'macOS 13+',
  url: 'https://orthant.app/',
  downloadUrl: 'https://orthant.app/download',
  license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  ...(version ? { softwareVersion: version } : {}),
};
---
<Base
  title="Orthant · Grid-based window manager for macOS"
  description="Snap the frontmost window with keyboard shortcuts, or summon a grid and draw exactly where it should go. Free and open source, for macOS 13+."
  path="/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  </Fragment>
  <Hero />
  <SpecStrip />

  <section class="section wrap" id="see-it-work">
    <h2>See it working</h2>
    <p class="lead-sub">
      Shortcuts, then the grid overlay, then drawing a region and binding it to a key.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-frame">
      <video
        controls muted playsinline loop
        preload="none"
        poster="/demo-poster.webp"
        width="1280"
        height="706"
        aria-label="Windows snap to halves and quarters with keyboard shortcuts; the grid overlay places a window on a drawn selection; a left-two-thirds region is drawn, saved, bound to a shortcut, and used."
      >
        <source src="/demo.webm" type="video/webm" />
        <source src="/demo.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>Draw any region</h2>
    <p class="lead-sub">
      A region stores the fraction it covers, not the cells you drew it on, so changing your grid
      later never moves your windows.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <RegionDiagram cols={3} rows={1} c0={0} c1={1} r0={0} r1={0}
          title="Two thirds of a three-column grid" label="2/3" />
        <p><strong>Draw it.</strong> Drag across the grid.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <RegionDiagram cols={12} rows={1} c0={0} c1={7} r0={0} r1={0}
          title="The same two thirds on a twelve-column grid" label="8/12" />
        <p><strong>It keeps its proportions.</strong> The same region on a finer grid.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <RegionDiagram cols={3} rows={1} c0={0} c1={1} r0={0} r1={0}
          title="Left two thirds, bound to a shortcut" label="2/3" combo="⌃⌥2" />
        <p><strong>Bind it.</strong> Press <kbd>⌘S</kbd> on the overlay, name it, give it a key.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p><a href="/docs/custom-regions/">How custom regions work →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2>Or just press a key</h2>
    <p class="lead-sub">Eleven shortcuts out of the box. Every one is rebindable.</p>
    <ul class="keymap">
      {BINDINGS.map((b) => (
        <li>
          {b.kind === 'block'
            ? <RegionDiagram cols={2} rows={2} {...b.block} title={b.label} />
            : <div class="no-glyph" aria-hidden="true">{b.kind === 'summon' ? 'grid' : 'as-is'}</div>}
          <span class="key-label">{b.label}</span>
          <kbd>{b.combo}</kbd>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="lead-sub">
      Center keeps the window at its current size, and Open grid places nothing at all, so neither
      has a shape to draw.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/docs/shortcuts/">Every default shortcut →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap prose">
    <h2>It stays out of the way</h2>
    <p>
      Orthant never becomes the frontmost app, so the window you are arranging stays active while
      you arrange it. It has no Dock icon, and it idles at <strong>0.0% CPU</strong> with
      0 wake-ups.
    </p>
    <p>
      A window snaps within its own display, not the one your pointer happens to be on. The
      overlay appears on every screen at once.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap prose">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <Command value="brew install --cask orthant-app/tap/orthant" />
    <p class="trust">
      Free and open source. Signed by the developer and notarized by Apple. No account, no
      telemetry in the app.
    </p>
    <p>
      Or <a href="/download">download the disk image</a> and drag Orthant to Applications. Either
      way you get the same build. Requires <strong>macOS 13+</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Orthant moves other apps' windows through the macOS Accessibility API, which macOS gates
      behind <strong>System Settings ▸ Privacy &amp; Security ▸ Accessibility</strong>. It is the
      only permission Orthant asks for, and first launch walks you through granting it.{' '}
      <a href="/docs/getting-started/">The full walkthrough →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Orthant updates itself in place, so <code>brew upgrade</code> deliberately skips it rather
      than fighting the built-in updater.
    </p>
    {version && (
      <p class="quiet">Latest release: <a href="/changelog/">{version}</a>.</p>
    )}
    <p class="quiet">
      Windows support is planned, behind the same interface. Linux is not possible: Wayland does
      not let one app move another's windows, by design.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .quiet { color: var(--label-secondary); }
  /* A "read more" link alone in its own paragraph is a standalone target, not a
     link inside a sentence, so WCAG 2.2's inline exemption does not cover it.
     20px tall before. */
  p > a:only-child { display: inline-block; padding-block: 3px; }
  .lead-sub { color: var(--label-secondary); margin: 0 0 24px; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .trust { color: var(--label-secondary); font-size: 15px; }
  video {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius); border: 1px solid var(--separator);
    background: var(--raised);
  }
  /* Positioning context for the fullscreen button demo.ts adds — only when a
     route for it actually exists (see that file). `position: relative` here,
     not `overflow: hidden`, so the button sits just inside the video's own
     rounded corner rather than being clipped by it. */
  .demo-frame { position: relative; }
  /*
   * `:global` because the button is created by demo.ts at runtime, the same
   * reason Command.astro styles its own JS-created `.copy` button this way —
   * the site's CSP (`style-src 'self'`) drops style attributes, so this is
   * authored here rather than written from the script.
   *
   * 36x36 clears the 24x24 WCAG 2.2 minimum on both axes with room to spare,
   * matching the touch-target floor the hero's grid cells were fixed to meet.
   */
  .demo-frame :global(.fs-btn) {
    position: absolute; top: 10px; right: 10px;
    width: 36px; height: 36px;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 0.18);
    background: rgb(16 22 34 / 0.6);
    color: #fff;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .demo-frame :global(.fs-btn:hover) { background: rgb(16 22 34 / 0.78); }

  .steps { display: grid; gap: 28px; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .steps { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .steps p { margin: 10px 0 0; color: var(--label-secondary); font-size: 15px; }

  .keymap {
    display: grid; gap: 20px 16px; list-style: none; margin: 0 0 20px; padding: 0;
    /* Two columns below 500px: eleven rows of one column each was a 2,250px
       wall on a 320px screen (measured before this change). auto-fit alone
       gives one column that narrow (two 128px tracks plus the gap exceed a
       272px content box) — minmax(0, …) so the forced 2-up track cannot be
       floored at its min-content width the way a bare `auto` track can. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
  @media (min-width: 500px) {
    .keymap { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(128px, 1fr)); gap: 20px 24px; }
  }
  .keymap li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 6px; }
  .keymap :global(.diagram) { width: 100%; max-width: 84px; }
  .key-label { font-size: 14px; }
  /* Center and the summon have no shape. The slot keeps its place in the grid
     so the map does not reflow, and says why rather than looking broken. */
  .no-glyph {
    width: 100%; max-width: 84px; aspect-ratio: 1;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule); border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 10px; color: var(--label-secondary);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { attachDemo } from '../lib/demo';
  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.demo-frame');
  if (root) attachDemo(root);
</script>
